    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-026-70140-9",
    "abstract": "The El Ni\u00f1o-Southern Oscillation (ENSO) is a leading mode of interannual climate variability with far-reaching global impacts. Understanding how ENSO-driven changes evolve in a warming climate is essential to project future climate variability. Here, we show that climate models robustly project an amplification of ENSO's influence on global sea surface temperature (SST) under greenhouse warming. This amplification is primarily driven by two factors: changes in El Ni\u00f1o-induced surface wind speed and alterations in the climatological air-sea humidity difference. The former is linked to enhanced atmospheric teleconnections associated with ENSO, while the latter stems from an overall increase in global SST. Our findings suggest that future El Ni\u00f1o events may exert stronger regional climate impacts, not only through intensified atmospheric teleconnections but also by reinforcing local air-sea interactions.",
    "field": "Climate dynamics",
    "problem": "Despite its critical role in global climate, limited attention has been given to understanding how El Ni\u00f1o-Southern Oscillation's (ENSO) influence on sea surface temperature in distant regions might respond to global warming, hindering projections of future climate variability.",
    "method": "This study uses climate model simulations and a mixed-layer heat budget equation to analyze and decompose the mechanisms responsible for the amplified impact of El Ni\u00f1o on global sea surface temperatures in a warming climate.",
    "task": "The task is to investigate the projected amplification of the El Ni\u00f1o-Southern Oscillation's (ENSO) influence on global sea surface temperature (SST) in a warming climate and to identify the underlying physical mechanisms.",
